        "id": 1288462
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-12T10:29:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "About two usb-c port case, there would be two scenarios:\n1.`it5271` chip * 2 for two c-port: These two chip have independently  i2c addresses, so each it52xx driver instance can operate independently, then we needn\u0027t this for loop.\n\n2.`it5272` chip * 1 for two c-port: Two c-port share one i2c address,  then I think we need MFD to do this. \n\nBut so far I use `it5271` to verify the PDC functions, about `it5272` it\u0027s another topic/patch later, now we could just focus on `it5271`. Thanks for your MFD information!\n\nSince we discuss about `it5272` case, I would like to ask a question, is it available to create only one c-port thread, but it actually controls two physical c-port?\nBecause two port use the same i2c address, it only can finish one UCSI command at a time, \nex.\n`port0`: write `[control]` -\u003e INT -\u003e idle -\u003e get `[CCI]` -\u003e write `[ACK_CC_CI]` -\u003e INT -\u003e idle -\u003e get `[CCI]` -\u003e finish\n`port1`: pending \u0026 wait port0 finish -\u003e write `[control]` .....\n\nAlso, for asynchronous interrupt (connector change indiacator), we need to get `[CCI]` first to know which `port` \u0026 `event` (`CC` or `CI`) assert the interrupt, then start to handle it.",
      "parentUuid": "367b8ed3_3578254b",
